WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.740
Isle Royale is an island in Lake Superior.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Wolves hunt moose there.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.700
In the winter of 2016, scientists counted only two wolves on the whole island.

4
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.540
They estimated 1,300 moose.

5
00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:21.860
So here is the question.

6
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.480
If wolves eat moose, why don't the wolves eat every last moose?

7
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:33.730
A population is all the members of one species living in one place.

8
00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.430
All the moose on the island are one population.

9
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:42.370
A predator is an animal that catches and eats other animals.

10
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.970
The animal it eats is its prey.

11
00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.610
Moose are the main prey of the island's wolves.

12
00:00:49.483 --> 00:00:52.203
A food web shows who eats whom.

13
00:00:53.043 --> 00:00:55.763
Each arrow points from the food to the eater.

14
00:00:56.983 --> 00:00:57.983
Moose eat fir trees.

15
00:00:58.943 --> 00:01:03.943
When a wolf eats a moose, some of the energy stored in the moose passes to the wolf.

16
00:01:05.303 --> 00:01:07.023
Wolves often hunt moose as a pack.

17
00:01:07.743 --> 00:01:11.143
A pack is a group of predators that live and hunt together.

18
00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:15.307
Here is a pattern scientists often see.

19
00:01:16.047 --> 00:01:19.567
When prey are plentiful, predators find food easily.

20
00:01:20.607 --> 00:01:22.747
More predators survive and raise young.

21
00:01:23.347 --> 00:01:25.147
So the predator population grows.

22
00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:31.467
Then more predators catch more prey, and the prey population falls.

23
00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:36.657
With less prey to eat, predators run short of food.

24
00:01:37.517 --> 00:01:40.937
Fewer of them survive, so the predator population falls.

25
00:01:42.037 --> 00:01:45.397
With fewer predators hunting, more prey survive.

26
00:01:46.257 --> 00:01:48.157
The prey population grows again.

27
00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:53.537
So predator numbers often rise and fall a little after prey numbers.

28
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.830
Real populations are messier.

29
00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:04.430
On Isle Royale, harsh winters, ticks, disease and the moose's plant food also change

30
00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:09.030
the counts. Predators are one cause of change, not the only one.

31
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:14.730
Still, predators usually run short of food before the last prey animal is caught.

32
00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:18.650
Then fewer wolves survive, and the moose can recover.

33
00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.610
That is why wolves do not eat every last moose.
